From 552d8b3d64bd0cb4302901b3fdcf50b5d4450fb2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jmgasper <jmgasper@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 18:21:42 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 07/14] zink/hgl: put the last frame back when a quiet window
 is uncovered

A window presented into the screen has nothing in the window system's
bitmap, so whatever covered it left a hole. The front buffer still holds
the last frame; this copies it back into the rectangles that have just
become visible.

Haiku tells a direct window it has been uncovered through
DirectConnected, not through a redraw request, so that is where it runs:
on the window's thread, with a command pool and fence of its own, never
touching the application's context. It only runs once the application
has been quiet for a tenth of a second - while it draws, its own next
frame repairs the window and its context owns the image.

Dropping the frame buffer after a mode change used to memset the whole
struct, which included the lock and the command buffer.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
